  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Spicy suillus (Suillus punctipes) is a small to medium-sized mushroom belonging to the Suillaceae family, characterized by its reddish-brown cap and distinctive dotted stem. This mycorrhizal fungus forms symbiotic relationships with coniferous trees, particularly larch and pine species.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Dotted Stem",
      "description": "The stem features dark reddish-brown punctate (dotted) markings on a paler background, which is the most distinctive identifying feature",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap Color",
      "description": "The cap ranges from reddish-brown to cinnamon-colored with a slightly viscid (sticky) surface when moist",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Pores and Tubes",
      "description": "The underside displays yellow to olive-yellow pores that may bruise bluish-green when handled",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Spicy suillus fruiting occurs in acidic soils beneath larch and pine trees in coniferous forests throughout New England, typically appearing in summer and early fall.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The 'spicy' common name may refer to the mushroom's slightly acrid taste, though like most wild mushrooms it should not be consumed without expert verification of edibility.",
